Employment depends on output, labor productivity, and a working time index, but is constrained by the available labor force.
12,137,0,134,602,102,35,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Working time index is 1 unless a WTR policy is active. This can be implemented independently or as part of a degrowth policy package.
12,138,0,1755,222,68,43,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Labor productivity, the working time index, and labor force determine the 'Labor potential output'.
12,139,0,887,834,82,54,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Labour force is proportional to population at the labour force participation rate, which decreases with unemployment rate (lagged) and is affected by climate damages on labour.
12,140,0,1160,736,70,43,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Unemployment is given by the difference between labor force and employment; the ratio to labor force gives the unemployment rate.
12,141,0,1310,357,66,33,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Labour productivity grows at an exogenous linear trend but is reduced by climate damages on labour.
12,142,0,1383,939,77,44,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Nominal wage increases with inflation, labour productivity and the complement of the unemployment rate. It can be increased exogenously by policy.
12,143,0,1367,137,66,33,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
The levels of employment and wages determine labour income.
12,144,0,834,347,81,30,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
When active, adaptation policies gradually reduce the impact of climate change on labour productivity
