12,274,0,2166,-738,136,54,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,256,0,0,0
There are 3 types of capital, used in the production of:|<<NnN>>|- Output (Capital O)|<<NnN>>|- Renewable energy (Capital ER)|<<NnN>>|- Recycled materials (Capital MR)|<<NnN>>||<<NnN>>|There are 3 corresponding types of investment as well.
12,275,0,2369,-552,83,36,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Capital stocks increase with investment (differentiated into3 types as well) and decrease with depreciation.
12,276,0,2549,38,83,36,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
The depreciation rate is the same for all types of capital, affected by climate damages and adaptation policy if active.
12,277,0,1860,225,62,35,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
All 3 types of investment are ultimetely constrained by the rationing rule.
12,278,0,1180,-576,96,42,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Investment O is proportional to output and follows an accelerator principle: the propensity to invest increases when capacity utilization is above normal capacity (exogenous).
12,279,0,1449,-771,102,41,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
The rate of capacity utilization is the ratio of final demand to potential output: measures how much of the installed productive capacity is being used.
12,280,0,1809,-629,69,44,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
The 'Capital potential output' is the maximum output that can be produced by using the total available stock of capital O, given an exogenous capital-output technical coefficient.
12,281,0,223,177,129,46,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Investment in renewable energy/recycling depends on source constraints (how close is output to its ER/MR potential level) and sink constraints (how close is temperature increase/waste to a given goal). A reaction exponent determines how fast is the reaction.
12,282,0,649,207,96,32,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
When the source or sink constraints approach, the desired share of ER/MR increases.
12,283,0,1136,-124,98,65,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
The required ER/MR investment (to meet the desired ER/MR share) is determined by the outstanding ER/MR capital stock, lagged output, and respective output intensities. Such investment is constrained by the energy/materials needs of the economy (no additional investment is required if there is no demand).
12,284,0,1467,-151,101,30,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Planned ER/MR investment is the required  one adjusted by an exogenous adjustment pace (how fast the required capital is built).
12,285,0,1434,221,60,44,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
ER/MR investment is constrained by a maximum level of investment increase.
12,286,0,1085,79,85,24,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Investment in renewable energy can be exogenously increased by policy.
12,287,0,3073,50,81,30,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
When active, adaptation policies gradually reduce the impact of climate change on capital depreciation
