12,120,0,701,204,80,40,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Output is demand-led but supply constrained: the minimum between final demand and potential output
12,121,0,847,450,77,45,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Demand-led: Final demand comes from households consumption, government spending, and investment (closed global economy).
12,122,0,700,326,94,37,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
When desired demand exceeds potential output, realized final demand is adjusted to the appropriate level by a rationing rule.
12,123,0,355,334,88,43,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Supply constraints: Potential output is the minimum between the highest technical levels of output given by available labor, capital, energy, and materials.
12,124,0,798,687,83,33,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Price of output set as a mark-up over unit costs (labour, debt service, and capital depreciation).
12,125,0,386,1002,95,55,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Scarcity impact on the price level: The mark-up is constant unless the rationing rule is active. In that case, mark-up increases proportionally and gradually returns to its base level when  supply constraint is not binding anymore.
12,126,0,89,906,58,31,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
A labor-market redistribution policy reduces the mark-up.
12,127,0,572,1284,98,39,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
Real values multiplied by the price give nominal values. Nominal output is split between wages and profits (functional distribution of income).
12,128,0,1273,1181,94,26,3,7,0,4,-1,0,0,0,0-0-0,255-255-128,|||0-0-0,0,0,0,0,0,0
What remains of profits after interest payments and debt repayment is distributed to capitalist households.
